{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the souls that do shall be cut off from among their people</b> — His seed is cut off and his days are cut off; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 17:9). Now there are in the karet punishments of the Torah three meanings. The first is where it says <b>\"that soul shall be cut off\"</b> (ibid. v. 4, 9); the second is where it says <b>\"the souls that do shall be cut off\"</b> (here), <b>\"that soul shall be cut off from before Me\"</b> (Leviticus 22:3); and the third is where it says <b>\"cut off shall that soul be; its iniquity is upon it\"</b> (Numbers 15:31). And concerning Yom Kippur He said (below 23:30), <b>\"I will destroy that soul from among its people\"</b>, and they said in Sifra (Emor ch. 14:4): since it says karet everywhere and I do not know what it is, when it says <b>\"I will destroy\"</b>, it teaches concerning karet that it is nothing other than destruction.\n\nThe explanation of the matter is: one who eats forbidden fat or blood, and he is righteous and most of his merits are many, but his desire overcame him and he stumbled in that transgression — his days are cut off and he dies in youth before reaching old age, which is sixty years, and his soul is not subject to karet; rather, he has a portion in the world of souls according to what is fitting for his good deeds, for he was righteous, and he further has a portion in the World to Come, which is the world after the resurrection. And concerning this it says <b>\"that man shall be cut off\"</b>. But one for whom, along with that grave sin, his iniquities are more numerous than his merits — the punishment of karet for the grave transgression reaches the sinning soul after it separates from the body, and it is cut off from the life of the world of souls. And concerning those liable for this karet the verse hints by saying <b>\"that soul shall be cut off from before Me\"</b>, and it is written <b>\"I will destroy that soul from among its people\"</b>. Yet these do not have karet in their body; sometimes they live and reach many days and even to old age and advanced years, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:15), <b>\"and there is a wicked man who prolongs his wickedness\"</b>. This is what our Rabbis said (see Rosh HaShanah 17a): but one whose iniquities are more numerous than his merits — and included among them is the iniquity of the wanton sinners of the nations in their bodies — and Rav Pappa said: for transgression, meaning for forbidden sexual relations from among the arayot, they descend to Gehinnom and are judged for twelve months; after twelve months their bodies perish and their souls are burned, and a wind scatters them under the soles of the righteous, etc.\n\nThere is also a severe karet, in which both body and soul are cut off; this is what is said concerning it (Numbers 15:31), <b>\"because he has despised the word of YHWH and broken His commandment — cut off shall that soul be; its iniquity is upon it\"</b>, and they interpreted (Shevuot 13a): <b>\"cut off\"</b> — in this world; <b>\"shall be cut off\"</b> — in the World to Come, meaning that he dies in youth and his beast lives in the holiness — that his soul will not live in the resurrection of the dead and has no portion in the World to Come. And the doubling of karet stated in the Torah appears only in matters of idolatry and blasphemy. And our Rabbis further interpreted it in tractate Shevuot (there): <b>\"because he has despised the word of YHWH\"</b> — this is one who casts off the yoke and shows contempt for the Torah; <b>\"and broken His commandment\"</b> — this is one who breaks the covenant of the flesh. But this does not apply to all karet punishments. And there it is explained in the Gemara that the doubling of <b>\"cut off shall be cut off\"</b> does not apply to the rest of those liable for karet in the Torah except to those derived from this verse, for the verse concerns a blasphemer and an idolater, and they added in the midrashim the heretics who deny the fundamental principle and the thoroughly wicked, as is said about them in the tradition (Isaiah 66:24), <b>\"for their worm shall not die and their fire shall not be quenched\"</b>, and they are those enumerated in the Mishnah (Sanhedrin 90a) and in the baraita (ibid. 105a) — and these are those who have no portion in the World to Come.\n\nSo too it appears that not all who are liable for karet for forbidden sexual relations have karet for their seed, but only those concerning whom it is written <b>\"they shall be childless (aririm)\"</b>. And it is possible that all the arayot were compared to one another. But regarding the other karet punishments, such as forbidden fat and blood, we have no such thing.\n\nIbn Ezra wrote (below 23:30): there is a difference between <b>\"I will destroy\"</b> and <b>\"shall be cut off\"</b>, but I cannot explain it. The wise man should consider that the destroyed one is lost and the cut-off one is extinguished, and Elohim will not bear the soul.\n\nYou shall know and understand that the karet punishments mentioned in connection with the soul constitute a great affirmation of faith in the endurance of souls after death and in the giving of reward in the world of souls. For when the Blessed One says <b>\"that soul shall be cut off from among its people\"</b>, <b>\"that soul shall be cut off from before Me\"</b>, it indicates that the sinning soul is the one cut off for its iniquity, while the rest of the souls that did not sin endure before Him in the supernal radiance. Therefore He specifies <b>\"that soul — its iniquity is upon it\"</b>, for the iniquity that is upon it is what cuts it off.\n\nThe reason for this is that the soul of man is the lamp of YHWH, which was breathed into our nostrils from the mouth of the Most High — the soul of Shaddai — as it is written (Genesis 2:7), <b>\"and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life\"</b>, and in its nature it does not die; it is not composite, so that its composition could be dissolved and it could have a cause of coming-to-be and passing-away like composite things. Rather, its existence is intrinsic, and it endures forever like the existence of the separate intelligences. Therefore the verse does not need to say that by virtue of the commandments its existence will endure, but rather that through the punishment of transgressions it becomes defiled and made impure and is cut off from the existence that is proper for it. This is the sense the Torah expresses with the term <i>karet</i>, like a branch cut off from the tree from which its root lives. This is what our Rabbis said (Sifrei Shelach 115): <b>\"from among its people\"</b> — and its people are at peace. For the karet of the sinning soul indicates the enduring existence of the other souls that did not sin, and they are its people who are in peace.\n\nWe have already explained (Exodus 6:2) that all the promises in the Torah, whether assurances or warnings, are all extraordinary, derived from hidden miracles, for through extraordinary matters the Torah gives promises and warnings to the world. Therefore here He warns concerning karet, which is a miraculous matter, and does not promise the existence that is natural. Now the karet punishments in the Torah are thirty-six (Keritot 2a), and many of them concern the prohibitions of forbidden sexual relations — I mean forbidden intercourse — and likewise capital punishments by the court in matters of intercourse are sixteen, while in the prohibition of forbidden foods there is no death at all.\n\nThe reason the great punishments, such as capital punishment by the court and karet, apply to matters of intercourse is that the uncovering of nakedness is a thing exceedingly abhorrent to the Torah, as mentioned in this portion and in many places in Scripture. And the Sages always mention (Sanhedrin 74a, and elsewhere) idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed — they mention it after idolatry and before bloodshed, and as they said (Sanhedrin 106a): their Elohim hates sexual immorality.\n\nThere is also for this matter a great secret in the secret of formation. And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:41), that because man's inclination regarding sexual intercourse is great and the desire for it is strong, and these are matters that are the source of many stumbling blocks, they require severe punishment to discipline them. And this too is true."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The reference to Rashi cites Leviticus 17:9; in standard editions Ramban's quotation of Rashi there reads 'his seed is cut off and his days are cut off'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:21.235974030Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}